libpamc tests
=============

Run the self-contained suite from the repository root, after Meson setup:

    meson compile -C build libpamc-tests
    meson test -C build --no-rebuild --suite libpamc --print-errorlogs

The alias builds all suite executables, including both agent helper variants.
Tests need neither installed PAM configuration nor root, a login session,
secret files, Perl, or external agent programs.  Agent tests use private
temporary directories and supervised subprocesses.  The supervisor bounds
execution time, reaps leftover descendants, and removes the directory tree
even when a test fails.  These tests do not use the legacy test/ demos.

Private fault-injection builds
-----------------------------

The normal API tests link the built shared library.  Additional private builds
replace selected calls only inside libpamc objects; they do not interpose on
libc, the driver, the supervisor, or the agent, or change the installed ABI.
The hook macros are installed after libc headers so fortified inline wrappers
cannot bypass injection.  The test harness does not disable fortification.

tst-pamc_converse-io forces short transfers, EINTR, and I/O failures.
tst-pamc_faults and tst-pamc_faults-ndebug first profile successful operations,
then fail each allocation API call in turn.  They verify rollback and recovery
using tracked live heap blocks, /proc/self/fd counts, and child reaping checks.
Startup cases cover both pipes, fork, both dup2 calls, exec, and interrupted
waitpid.  Child startup failures are reported at pamc_end(), not pamc_load().
Shared fault reports verify the child's PID, injection kind and call ordinal,
not merely an unsuccessful exit status.  A separate shared case label lets
the supervisor report the failing scenario even after a worker crash or
timeout, without adding output to successful runs.

Real-descriptor tests cover every nonempty combination of closed stdin,
stdout and stderr, requiring pipe remapping to preserve those closed slots.
Low descriptor limits also exercise relocation failure and rollback.  I/O
tests inject errors after partial header/body writes; the agent independently
checks exactly which prefix arrived before EOF.  The I/O hooks use libc
read/write so sanitizer buffer checks remain active.

Prompt allocation failure intentionally triggers PAM_BP_ASSERT in debug
builds; tst-pam_bp covers that assertion policy.  The NDEBUG fault build also
checks one-shot and persistent reply/fallback OOM without terminating the
process.  Assertions in the test driver itself remain active in both modes.

Sanitizers
----------

To instrument the shared library, all private builds, drivers, and agents:

    meson setup build-pamc-sanitize -Db_sanitize=address,undefined -Doptimization=1
    meson compile -C build-pamc-sanitize libpamc-tests
    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
        meson test -C build-pamc-sanitize --no-rebuild --suite libpamc --print-errorlogs

Then exercise the shared library with assertions disabled as well:

    meson configure build-pamc-sanitize -Db_ndebug=true
    meson compile -C build-pamc-sanitize libpamc-tests
    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
        meson test -C build-pamc-sanitize --no-rebuild --suite libpamc --print-errorlogs

With Clang, Meson may also require -Db_lundef=false for sanitizer builds.
The header-macro and fault tests always include explicit builds of both
assertion policies regardless of the project-wide b_ndebug setting.  The
commands above additionally exercise the shared library under both
policies, without requiring CI configuration changes.  Hostile reply-size
tests send only a header for invalid lengths and never allocate huge bodies.
